<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maryland State Colonization Society</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> State Colonization Society was a branch of the American colonisation movement that from 1831 organised the emigration of free Black Americans from Maryland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with funds from the state legislature. In 1834 it founded a colony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-palmas">Cape Palmas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, with James Hall as its first agent. The colony became the independent Republic of Maryland in 1854 and joined Liberia in 1857.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maryland-state-colonization-society.jpg" alt="Maryland State Colonization Society" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maryland State Colonization Society · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maryland_in_Liberia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The society was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, founded in 1816 to send free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. A Maryland Colonization Society was founded in 1827 with the wealthy planter Charles Carroll of Carrollton, a substantial slaveholder, as its first president; he supported the gradual abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and introduced a bill for it in the Maryland senate, which did not pass, but he kept his own slaves. Many planters belonged, among them the Steuart family, including George H. Steuart, a major general of the Maryland Militia. The society presented itself as a remedy for slavery, and it was founded in part as a response to the threat of slave rebellion, such as that of Nat Turner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> in 1831.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In December 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> legislature voted funds, up to a total of $260,000 over 26 years, to transport free Black people and former slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and empowered the society to carry out the plan. Settlers were offered free passage, rent, 5 acres of land and loans. At the same time freed slaves were required to leave the state unless a court allowed them to stay, and in 1832 free Black people were barred from voting, serving on juries and holding office, to encourage them to emigrate. John Latrobe, the society's president for two decades, declared that sooner or later every free person of colour would be persuaded to leave Maryland.</p><p>The settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-palmas">Cape Palmas</a>, a small rocky peninsula south of the rest of Liberia, was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> on 12 February 1834. In 1836 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-brown-russwurm">John Brown Russwurm</a> became its first Black governor and held the post until his death in 1851. The colony stayed out of the Commonwealth of Liberia, as the society wished to keep its trade monopoly, and became the State of Maryland on 2 February 1841. It declared independence on 29 May 1854, with its capital at Harper. Attacked by the Grebo and the Kru, it called on Liberia for help, and on 18 March 1857 it was annexed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland-county">Maryland County</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the 1850s few Marylanders still believed that colonisation would solve the problem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, which ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> only with the Union's victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> in 1865. The society did not become the remedy it had promised, but it contributed to the making of the modern state of Liberia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, of which it was a branch, was formally dissolved in 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maryland State Colonization Society” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Maryland State Colonization Society</b>, founded to remove free Black people from Maryland, sent settlers in 1834 under its agent James Hall.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-maryland">Republic of Maryland</a></li></ul></section>
